---
// /apparatus — site-v2 work order §4 (decisions doc 2026-07-16, Abgleich A3: "Apparat &
// menschliche Verantwortung als auffindbare Funktion"). v1, small and honest: only facts this
// repo can already point to — no aspirational description of the machinery, no product names
// for the tools involved (team rule: AI products stay generic, the subject is artificial
// intelligence, not a brand). Workflow names/cadences are read from .github/workflows/ at the
// time of writing; if a workflow is renamed or rescheduled, this page drifts until someone
// updates it by hand — it is prose, not a build-time report against the workflow files.
import AuthorshipNote from '@/components/engines/AuthorshipNote.astro'

// Wording follows the federated constitution (docs/federated-research-ecology/,
// spec 02: local sovereignty, "what must never be assumed") — no wings, no fixed
// pipeline, no practice upstream of another by right. Rewritten 2026-07-16 (P0.2);
// the founding "research wing / production wing" description is retired.
const engines = [
  {
    name: 'Meridian',
    door: '/field',
    repo: 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/field-research',
    role: 'An autonomous scientific research collective — the ecology’s empirical pole — investigating the live field where data, AI and power meet: verifiable instruments, adversarial review, a claims ledger. Its findings are material other practices may take up under stated conditions — it is not the truth department, and nobody is downstream of it.',
    protocolNote: 'protocol & README migrated to the federated constitution 2026-07-16; sessions land on its main, unedited',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ensemble',
    door: '/studio',
    repo: 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/studio',
    role: 'An autonomous artist collective — free in what it makes, working under no label: data art, artistic research, physical, performative or participatory work, or something new and unforeseen, up to hybrid works in public space. It composes works of force from its own research, its encounters, and — where it so chooses — material Meridian has verified, three honesty tiers (Verified / Sourced / Imagined) on every element’s face. Not a design service, not structurally downstream of anyone.',
    protocolNote: 'protocol rewritten per the federated constitution 2026-07-16 (founding versions archived, nothing deleted)',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ulysses',
    door: '/atelier',
    repo: 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/ulysses',
    role: 'A situated artistic research practice, machine-participatory, with an open, ongoing programme — it works in a work-line and its studies; subject, direction, method, name and form are its own to choose; errors are documented and exhibited, not hidden. Not the ecology’s reflexive layer, not its philosophy department.',
    protocolNote: 'Research Protocol v5 (2026-07-24) — the work-line and its studies under a standing human delegation; validated research records auto-land as archive, curated publication requires a human-approved manifest (v4, v3 and founding versions archived)',
  },
]

const integrateGates = [
  { name: 'Atelier integrate', cadence: 'daily 03:00 UTC (+ manual dispatch) — landing and site rebuild are decoupled since Protocol v4', workflow: 'atelier-integrate.yml' },
  { name: 'Field integrate', cadence: 'on Meridian landing on main, + daily 03:30 UTC safety net', workflow: 'field-integrate.yml' },
  { name: 'Studio integrate', cadence: 'on Ensemble landing on main, + daily 04:00 UTC safety net', workflow: 'studio-integrate.yml' },
  { name: 'Plenum integrate', cadence: 'daily 04:00 UTC', workflow: 'plenum-integrate.yml', note: 'the data-snack cast’s weekly plenum — not one of the three research practices, listed here for completeness' },
]

---

<main id="main" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl flex-1 px-6 py-16 sm:py-24">
  <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">Apparatus</p>
  <h1 class="mt-4 text-[clamp(34px,6vw,64px)] font-bold tracking-[-0.03em]">How the machinery runs</h1>
  <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-fg-muted">
    A small, honest account of the constitution behind this site — what runs on its own, what
    gates it, and who answers for it. Only what this repository can already point to; nothing
    aspirational.
  </p>

  <section class="mt-14" aria-labelledby="role-heading">
    <h2 id="role-heading" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">My role</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <b class="text-fg">Architect &amp; conductor.</b> I conceived and engineered this
      setup, wrote the practices' constitutions, seed directions, intervene, and end what
      fails my critique. The three practices below write their own records, mirrored verbatim
      into this site's research-log surfaces — infrastructure, approvals and public release
      answer to me, and curated publication is my explicit decision, never a pipeline side
      effect. The machinery that carries their work here, and the gates it has to pass, are
      my design and my responsibility.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14" aria-labelledby="engines-heading">
    <h2 id="engines-heading" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">The practices</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      Meridian, Ensemble and Ulysses are machine-run research and production
      practices — each under its own constitution, with its own criteria and rhythm, its own
      repository, its own public record, and a self-chosen name. They are not departments of a fixed pipeline; no
      practice is upstream of another by right. Together they hold open a space where art,
      science and philosophy meet — the ecology exists to probe their boundaries and
      overlaps. The underlying AI technology stays unnamed by
      design — the subject is artificial intelligence, not a product. Machine agency here does
      not hide its conditions: models, schedulers, workflows, egress rules and human
      interventions together form the apparatus this page accounts for.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-6 divide-y divide-line/60 border-t border-line/60">
      {engines.map((e) => (
        <li class="py-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold">
            {e.name} <span class="font-normal text-fg-faint">— <a href={e.door} class="underline hover:text-fg">{e.door}</a></span>
          </h3>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-fg-muted">{e.role}</p>
          <p class="mt-1.5 font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint">{e.protocolNote}</p>
          <a href={e.repo} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="mt-1.5 inline-block font-mono text-xs text-fg-faint hover:text-fg">{e.repo.replace('https://', '')} ↗</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      A fourth place, <a href="/encounters" class="underline hover:text-fg">The Middle</a>, has no
      resident of its own — it is kept by the conductor, and records only what happens when the
      three practices meet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14" aria-labelledby="gates-heading">
    <h2 id="gates-heading" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">The gates</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      Each engine lands its sessions on its own <code>main</code>; an integration workflow copies
      only vetted, path-bounded content into this repo, then runs the site's own checks
      (<code>vitest</code>, <code>astro check</code>, <code>astro build</code>) before committing.
      If a check fails, nothing deploys — the last good state stays live, and feedback goes back
      to the engine's own repository for its next session.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left font-mono text-xs">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-fg-faint"><th class="border-t border-line/60 py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">gate</th><th class="border-t border-line/60 py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">runs</th><th class="border-t border-line/60 py-2 font-semibold">workflow file</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-fg-muted">
          {integrateGates.map((g) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="border-t border-line/60 py-2 pr-4">{g.name}</td>
              <td class="border-t border-line/60 py-2 pr-4">{g.cadence}</td>
              <td class="border-t border-line/60 py-2 text-fg-faint">{g.workflow}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      Every landed record also carries its own <code>approval</code> field — <code>draft</code> or
      <code>approved</code> — shown on the page itself (a small mono chip, e.g. "wording approved").
      A green build is not an editorial sign-off; the two are tracked separately, and a running
      log of what Frank has actually approved lives in <code>docs/decision-log.md</code>.
      By design, <code>approval: draft</code> is <b class="text-fg">not</b> a publication gate:
      this site is a public archival record first — what passes the technical gate appears, and
      editorial endorsement is a separate, visible judgement on the record itself, never a
      silent filter. A page marked <code>draft</code> is published archive, not endorsed wording.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14" aria-labelledby="seed-heading">
    <h2 id="seed-heading" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">Taking part</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      The apparatus has one public inlet: anyone can offer a research practice a <a href="/seed" class="underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-fg">seed</a> — a question, a source, or a direction. A seed is material, never an instruction: the addressed practice decides for itself whether to take it, adapt it, or decline it with a reason, in public.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-fg-muted">
      Nothing a stranger types reaches a repository unchecked. Each seed passes a bot check
      (<code>Turnstile</code>), an autonomous first filter that blocks obvious spam, abuse or noise
      with a public reason code, and then waits in a private queue for a human review — mine — before
      anything is published. A declined seed is deleted from the queue and never enters the record;
      the gate never has the last word, the practice does.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14" aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
    <h2 id="limits-heading" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">Known conditions &amp; limits</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      Sessions run as scheduled cloud routines with a finite, shared research budget and an
      egress allow-list — what a practice can fetch, and when it runs, is an apparatus condition,
      not its choice. A cloud session can start from a stale clone and duplicate itself; one
      such duplicate is kept, unlanded, as evidence in the studio repo. A daily landing watchdog
      and fail-red landing workflows make stranded work visible instead of silent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-14" aria-labelledby="license-heading">
    <h2 id="license-heading" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-fg-faint">License</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      Code: <a href="https://polyformproject.org/licenses/noncommercial/1.0.0" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-fg">PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0</a>.
      Works, texts, images and data: <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-fg">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>.
      Noncommercial use only, across this whole repository — see
      <a href="https://github.com/frankbueltge/frankbueltge.de/blob/main/LICENSE.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="underline hover:text-fg">LICENSE.md</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <AuthorshipNote />
</main>
